				<h1>Run multiple Codex CLI agents in git worktrees</h1>
				<div class="post-meta">By Minh Ngo · Published <time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time> · Updated <time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time></div>
				<p class="lede">To run multiple Codex CLI agents safely, start each one in its own git worktree so their edits stay on separate branches. Anywhere, a free MIT Mac app, creates the worktree from a branch or pull request, runs Codex in a terminal or as a chat, and flags each session as Working, Needs you, Done or Failed.</p>
				<p>This is the Codex version of <a href="/blog/run-multiple-claude-code-agents-in-parallel">How to run multiple Claude Code agents in parallel</a>. The method is the same; the Codex-specific parts are below.</p>

				<h2 id="why-one-worktree-per-agent">Why one worktree per Codex agent</h2>
				<p>Two <code>codex</code> sessions in the same folder share one working tree and one branch. Each sees the other's half-written files in its builds and tests, a commit from one can sweep up the other's edits, and the final diff mixes both tasks. A git worktree gives each agent its own checkout on its own branch, from the same repository, without a second clone.</p>

				<h2 id="plain-git">Plain git first</h2>
				<p>Add a worktree per task, then start Codex in each folder, in its own terminal:</p>
				<pre><code>cd ~/code/app
git worktree add -b fix-auth ../app-fix-auth
git worktree add -b flaky-tests ../app-flaky-tests

cd ~/code/app-fix-auth &amp;&amp; codex
cd ~/code/app-flaky-tests &amp;&amp; codex</code></pre>
				<p>A new worktree holds only committed files, so install dependencies and copy untracked config before Codex starts. When a branch is merged, <code>git worktree remove</code> its folder.</p>
				<p>Codex changes between releases. Check the <a href="https://developers.openai.com/codex/cli">Codex CLI docs</a> for any worktree support built into the version you run. The commands above work with any version.</p>
				<p>What git doesn't give you is an overview: which Codex is waiting on an approval, which one finished, and what each one changed. That's the part Anywhere takes over.</p>

				<h2 id="in-anywhere">Running several Codex agents in Anywhere</h2>

				<h3>Set up Codex once</h3>
				<ol>
					<li>Install the Codex CLI from the <a href="https://developers.openai.com/codex/cli">Codex CLI install page</a> and run <code>codex login</code>. Anywhere runs the <code>codex</code> it finds on your login <code>PATH</code>. If you install it while Anywhere is open, restart Anywhere.</li>
					<li>To make Codex the agent new sessions start with, open Settings › <strong>Agents</strong>, click Codex under <strong>Providers</strong>, and click <strong>Set as default</strong>. Or pick it per session on the new session page.</li>
					<li>Settings › <strong>Accounts</strong> can hold more than one Codex account, and shows each account's usage.</li>
				</ol>

				<h3>Terminal or chat</h3>
				<p>Codex runs both ways, and reports its status from both:</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Chat.</strong> Anywhere's own chat view. pocketd drives Codex through the Codex app-server. New sessions start as a chat while Settings › <strong>Agents</strong> › <strong>Start as a chat</strong> is on, the default.</li>
					<li><strong>Terminal.</strong> Codex's own terminal interface, in your login shell. Anywhere reads its status from Codex session events. Turn <strong>Start as a chat</strong> off, or start Codex from a tab's <strong>+</strong> menu.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>One caveat from the docs: a <code>codex</code> that runs without its app-server isn't attached, and then Anywhere shows only its terminal activity, never Needs you or Done.</p>

				<h3>A worktree per session, from a branch or a pull request</h3>
				<p>Press <kbd>⌘⇧N</kbd> and the new session page opens with <strong>Worktree</strong> picked. Pick the base branch on the branch chip, or click the pull request button and enter <code>123</code>, <code>#123</code>, or a pull request URL to start from that pull request. Setup commands in <code>.anywhere/config.json</code> or <code>.anywhere/setup.sh</code> run in the new worktree before Codex starts. Worktrees go in <code>~/.anywhere/worktrees</code> by default. See <a href="/docs/worktrees">Start an agent in a new worktree</a>.</p>
				<p>From a shell, the same thing is one command:</p>
				<pre><code>anywhere session create --new-worktree --base main --provider codex --prompt "Fix the flaky login test"</code></pre>

				<h3>Status, review, and access</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>Each session is <span class="status working">Working</span>, <span class="status needs-you">Needs you</span>, <span class="status done">Done</span>, <span class="status failed">Failed</span>, or <span class="status">Idle</span>. Needs you, Failed, and Done reach the sidebar, the Inbox, the Dock badge, and banners. <kbd>⌘J</kbd> jumps to the next session that needs you. See <a href="/docs/sessions">Sessions and status</a>.</li>
					<li>The <strong>Changes</strong> panel shows each worktree's diff beside its session, and can compare it with the base branch. The pull request card's <strong>Send</strong> hands a failed check's log or a review comment back to Codex.</li>
					<li>The access chip sets what Codex can do without asking: <strong>Ask</strong>, <strong>Edits</strong>, <strong>Auto</strong>, or <strong>Full</strong>, or Codex's own settings. Codex doesn't start in plan mode.</li>
				</ul>

				<div class="cta">
					<p>Anywhere is free and MIT licensed, and runs the Codex you already have.</p>
					<a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/mingo023/anywhere">Star Anywhere on GitHub</a>
