				</div>

				<h2 id="codex-and-claude-code">Codex and Claude Code side by side on one repo</h2>
				<p>Codex and Claude Code are the two agents that report their status from a terminal as well as from a chat. Mixing them on one project works the same as running two of either:</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Separate worktrees.</strong> Start a Codex session in one worktree and a Claude Code session in another. Both sit under the same project in the sidebar, in tabs and splits.</li>
					<li><strong>Delegation.</strong> A Claude Code or Codex chat can hand work to sessions of another agent through pocketd's orchestrator. Ask in plain words, for example "Split this into three parts and delegate each one to its own session. Use Codex for the tests." Each delegated session runs in a new worktree made from the parent's latest commit, with the parent's access. See <a href="/docs/delegation">Let a chat delegate work to other sessions</a>.</li>
					<li><strong>The Board.</strong> Write tickets and give each its own agent. A started ticket gets its own worktree and session, and the Board runs up to four at once by default. See <a href="/docs/board">Plan work on the Board</a>.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>The other eight agents Anywhere supports run as a chat. The full list, with install and sign-in commands, is on <a href="/docs/agents">Supported agents</a>. To drive all of this from scripts, see <a href="/blog/script-claude-code-codex-from-cli">Script coding agents from your shell</a>.</p>

				<section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq">
					<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
					<h3>Can I run several Codex agents on one repository?</h3>
					<p>To run multiple Codex CLI agents safely, start each one in its own git worktree so their edits stay on separate branches. Anywhere, a free MIT Mac app, creates the worktree from a branch or pull request, runs Codex in a terminal or as a chat, and flags each session as Working, Needs you, Done or Failed.</p>
					<h3>Can Codex and Claude Code work on the same project at once?</h3>
					<p>Yes. Give each agent its own worktree of the project. In Anywhere, pick the agent on the new session page for each one. Both run in a terminal or as a chat and report their status from either, and a Claude Code or Codex chat can delegate part of its work to a session of the other agent.</p>
					<h3>How do I know when a Codex agent is done?</h3>
					<p>Anywhere marks the session Done when a Codex turn ends and you haven't seen it yet, and shows it in the Inbox, on the Dock badge and in a macOS banner. Codex reports its status from a chat and from a terminal. From a script, anywhere session wait exits with code 0 when the session is done.</p>
				</section>
